// Prisma 7 schema root. This file carries the `generator` and `datasource`
// blocks and nothing else; the models live beside it in `models/`, one file per
// concern. Prisma's multi-file schema is selected by `schema: "prisma"` in
// `prisma.config.ts`, which names the *directory* — every `.prisma` file under
// it is part of one schema, and a relation may cross files freely.
//
// Two constraints fix that shape, and both are why `schema` points at `prisma`
// rather than at a nested `prisma/schema`:
//
//   * The file holding the `generator` block has to sit at the root of the
//     schema directory, so this file cannot move into `models/`.
//   * `migrations` has to sit beside that root. Pointing the schema one level
//     deeper would force the migration history to move with it, rewriting
//     paths that three applied migrations already depend on.
//
// `output` is resolved relative to this file, so the split leaves it correct.
// `importFileExtension = ""` is required because the generated client is
// imported from TypeScript compiled by Next.js, which resolves extensionless
// specifiers.
//
// The datasource deliberately carries no `url`. The CLI's connection string
// lives in `prisma.config.ts` and is `DIRECT_URL`, because a migration must
// never run through a transaction pooler; the runtime client's is
// `DATABASE_URL`, read by the `@prisma/adapter-pg` driver adapter in
// `src/lib/db.ts`. Locally the two are the same string, on Supabase they are
// not. See `docs/adr/0003-local-postgres-development-supabase-deployment.md`.

generator client {
  provider            = "prisma-client"
  output              = "../src/generated/prisma"
  importFileExtension = ""
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}
